# Shared Practices

- Keep configuration explicit: document required env/TOML keys near usage, fail fast when missing, never commit secrets.
- Align local and production config shapes; prefer safe defaults.
- Tests stay fast and deterministic; add/update unit tests when behavior changes; run integration tests only when touching infra boundaries.
- Prefer small, table-style cases for services/stores where useful.
- Update existing docs/config examples when API or config shapes change; keep notes brief.

## Code Review Guidelines
- Review changes for security implications and performance impact
- Ensure new code follows established patterns and architecture principles
- Check for proper error handling and input validation
- Verify tests are included for new functionality
- Confirm documentation is updated for API/behavior changes

## Git Workflow
- Use descriptive commit messages following conventional format
- Keep commits focused and atomic; avoid large, multi-purpose commits
- Use feature branches for new development; merge via pull requests
- Run tests and linting before pushing changes
- Squash commits when merging to maintain clean history

## Dependency Management
- Regularly update dependencies for security patches and bug fixes
- Evaluate new dependencies critically; prefer small, focused libraries
- Use lockfiles (go.sum, package-lock.json) to ensure reproducible builds
- Audit dependencies for vulnerabilities using automated tools
- Remove unused dependencies to keep the project lean

## Monitoring & Alerting
- Implement health checks for all services and external dependencies
- Set up alerts for critical failures (database connectivity, message processing)
- Monitor performance metrics (response times, error rates, resource usage)
- Use structured logging with correlation IDs for request tracing
- Establish runbooks for common incident response scenarios
